  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})=$",
  "worked_solution": "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{9}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
